Knowledge-intensive reasoning systems increasingly rely on reflective steps such as self-evaluation, error diagnosis, and plan revision to organize verification workflows. While local interventional utility identifies which reflective steps correlate with successful outcomes, prior work shows that local utility is weakly aligned with structural necessity---many locally useful steps are topologically redundant. This paper presents Structurally-Calibrated Functional Attribution (SC-FMA), a claim-bounded methodology for auditable verification-step weighting in knowledge-intensive reasoning systems. SC-FMA converts a coarse utility or proxy fidelity input into calibrated supervision weights through constrained convex optimization, without treating that input as a fully step-discriminative oracle. The core contribution is the Structurally-Calibrated Utility (SCU) objective, which jointly optimizes fidelity to this utility anchor, consistency with graph-level structural necessity, redundancy reduction, and bottleneck protection. The objective is strictly convex, guaranteeing a unique global solution, and provides formal monotonicity and variance-reduction guarantees conditional on the supplied inputs. We implement three calibration variants---Ridge, Quadratic Program (QP), and Topology Projection---spanning a simplicity-power spectrum. On a controlled synthetic step-importance benchmark, SC-FMA QP achieves a mean Spearman $\rho$ of 0.608 with controlled proxy step labels, outperforming raw CIU (0.483), gradient attribution (0.491), Monte Carlo Shapley (0.524), and information-theoretic baselines (0.310). On PRM800K real process-supervision data (4{,}417 samples, 34{,}219 labeled steps under a frozen hash split), the primary positive signal is the stored \texttt{w\_struct} step ranking ($\rho = 0.611$); Ridge closely preserves this ordering ($\rho = 0.604$), while QP ($\rho = 0.442$) and Projection ($\rho = -0.135$) are downgraded for this real-data route. A bounded PRM800K audit-prioritization readout provides moderate, preliminary real-data support: the same locked-split evidence can concentrate high-rated process steps under a fixed review budget, but only as offline audit context for PRM800K-like process annotations. SC-FMA provides a principled bridge from intervention-based attribution to process-supervision-oriented analysis, while downstream PRM training, task-specific replay validation, and production KBS deployment remain future validation questions.
